<!--
    Jump thunks named after the function they jump to
    (`option thunknames on|off`, default on).

    A function whose first instruction is a direct unconditional branch is a
    jump thunk: a linker branch island, an identical-code-folding forwarder,
    an outlined tail call. Without a symbol of its own it stays
    `sub_<addr>`, and every call through it is anonymous although its target
    is named.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 environment-binding.
    kuna-console engine/kuna_thunknames.rs decodes the first instruction of
    every discovered entry and, after every other name source, renames a
    placeholder thunk `j_<target>`; a thunk to a thunk becomes `j_j_<target>`.
    Each thunk also takes its target's prototype, so the callers' first
    argument is typed `long *` from release_box's DWARF.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture. Both passes clear
    it, load thunknames_aarch64 and take the `mode fast` inventory. The
    fixture is a static AArch64 ELF built from thunknames_aarch64.c:
    release_box(long *, long) has a DWARF prototype, the unnamed thunk at
    0x21019c is `b release_box`, the one at 0x2101a0 is `b 0x21019c`, and
    drop_box / drop_two call them with bl, passing their own first argument.

    Pass 1 (`option thunknames off`) reproduces the bug: both calls render
    sub_<addr>() and the callers' argument stays untyped. Pass 2 is the
    shipped default.

    Passes 3 and 4 (off, then the default) load thunknames_mips, built from
    thunknames_mips.S: two symbol-less `j release_box` entries, 0x4000e0
    with a nop in its delay slot and 0x4000e8 with `addiu $a0,$a0,16`
    there. The delay slot is part of the branch, so only the first is a
    thunk; the second adjusts its argument and keeps its placeholder.

    Assertions span the combined output of both passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c0035fd6
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
